<section class="three-tool-section" id="hawl-meaning"><h3>What does hawl mean in Zakat?</h3>
<p><em>Hawl</em> is the passage of an Islamic lunar year relevant to many types of zakatable wealth. The Quran directs believers to pay Zakat, while classical hadith literature and scholarly jurisprudence explain the wealth thresholds and year condition; see <a href="https://quran.com/9/60" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Quran 9:60</a> and <a href="https://sunnah.com/abudawud:1573" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Sunan Abi Dawud 1573</a> for a report discussing a year and classical amounts (its transmission and application warrant qualified review).</p>
<p>A lunar year generally runs around 354 or 355 civil days. Because the Gregorian calendar is solar, a Hijri anniversary shifts earlier on the Gregorian calendar. Simply choosing the same date next year—for example, the same day in March—is not a reliable hawl calculation.</p></section>
<section class="three-tool-section" id="hawl-start"><h3>When does your Zakat year begin?</h3>
<p>The starting point generally relates to when relevant owned wealth first met the applicable <a href="/tools/nisab-calculator/">Nisab</a>, but different schools consider changes in holdings or dips below the threshold differently. The <a href="https://www.islamic-relief.org.uk/giving/islamic-giving/zakat/when-to-pay-zakat/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Islamic Relief guide to when Zakat is due</a> describes an anniversary-based review and practical recordkeeping.</p>
<p>For income, inheritance, a new gold holding or recently purchased investment, the fact that you bought or received that asset on a certain day does not automatically mean a brand-new, independent year begins for every item. Get guidance on hawl continuity and aggregation, particularly when your wealth fluctuated, Nisab was disputed, or you owe past payments.</p>
<p>Use this planner only after identifying a reference date under your chosen scholarly approach. It is designed for people who want a convenient recurring <strong>review reminder</strong>, rather than religious eligibility automation.</p></section>
<section class="three-tool-section" id="hawl-calculation"><h3>How the Hijri anniversary is calculated</h3>
<ol class="three-tool-steps"><li>Enter a past Gregorian civil reference date supported by the Umm al-Qura calendar.</li><li>The tool converts that date into the equivalent numbered Hijri month and day.</li><li>It advances the Hijri year, finds each corresponding civil date and skips dates already in the past.</li><li>If day 30 of a particular month does not exist in a future lunar year, the tool uses day 29 and clearly labels the substitution.</li><li>Optionally view a ±1 civil-day illustration. This is not a prediction of a moon-sighting announcement or a claim of religious accuracy.</li><li>Download all-day calendar markers for the next one, three or five reviews, then confirm your actual obligation independently.</li></ol>
<p>The tool's time and date conversion is based on the platform's <code>Intl</code> Umm al-Qura calendar support; other calendar systems and community announcements may differ. Local Islamic days start at sunset, whereas the downloadable markers use a <strong>civil all-day date</strong>. A reminder has no automatic sunset alert.</p></section>
<section class="three-tool-section" id="hawl-example"><h3>Worked example: why a lunar-year anniversary moves earlier</h3>
<div class="three-tool-example"><p>Suppose a qualified assessment establishes that a person's initial Zakat review date corresponds to <strong>10 Ramadan 1447 AH</strong>. Their next annual review target under the same calendar convention is <strong>10 Ramadan 1448 AH</strong>, not automatically the same Gregorian date twelve solar months later.</p><p>The precise Gregorian day is calculated by the tool, and a local moon-sighting announcement could differ. This example does not establish that the person was over Nisab or that Zakat became due.</p></div>
<p>If a different lunar month has only 29 days in one year, a prior 30th-day reference does not map directly; the calculator labels its fallback instead of silently moving or displaying an impossible date. The planned date should still be reviewed with a qualified authority.</p></section>
<section class="three-tool-section" id="hawl-calendar"><h3>Calendar reminders, overdue years and data privacy</h3>
<p>The downloaded <code>.ics</code> file contains annual all-day events marked as estimated Zakat reviews. Open it in a calendar application to organise a wealth assessment. It does <strong>not</strong> demand payment, establish arrears or contain balances, bank accounts or investment information. The separate text export provides a list of illustrative dates.</p>
<p>If you have not assessed wealth for several years, do not multiply today's total by the years in this tool. Your historical balances, each applicable year, asset treatments, the selected benchmark and any prior payments need to be considered. Use the <a href="/tools/zakat-calculator/">main Zakat Calculator</a> for present-day arithmetic and obtain qualified guidance for arrears.</p></section>
<section class="three-tool-section" id="hawl-compare"><h3>Nisab, hawl and Zakat amount: three different decisions</h3>
<div class="three-tool-comparison"><div><h4>Nisab</h4><p>The relevant minimum qualifying wealth reference; gold and silver comparisons can differ.</p></div><div><h4>Hawl</h4><p>Time and continuity conditions relevant to the applicable wealth; this tool only plots dates.</p></div><div><h4>Qualifying wealth</h4><p>Cash, business stock, metals and investments require appropriate classifications and deductions.</p></div><div><h4>Amount to arrange</h4><p>Typically 2.5% of eligible net wealth after all relevant conditions are established.</p></div></div></section>
